---
import Prose from '../../layouts/Prose.astro';
import { getDocsPage } from '../../data/docs-topics';
import release from '../../data/release.json';
import { getReleaseCommands, getReleaseNames } from '../../lib/release-names';

const page = getDocsPage('cli');
const names = getReleaseNames(release);
const commands = getReleaseCommands(names);
---
<Prose title={page.title} description={page.description} path={page.path}>
  <p>The <code>{names.command}</code> command opens files in the running app. The Windows installer and the <code>.deb</code> put it on your PATH, and <code>install.sh</code> puts it in <code>~/.local/bin</code>. On macOS or from a downloaded AppImage, install it from Settings, Integrations, Terminal command (macOS asks for an administrator password once when <code>/usr/local/bin</code> is not writable).{names.isCurrentName && <Fragment> <code>writ</code> runs the same command: the installers add both names, and the Settings action keeps a <code>writ</code> link that an earlier version made.</Fragment>}</p>
  <pre><code>{commands.cliExamples}</code></pre>
  <p>Piped text is saved as a file in Jotra's folder, so it stays after the terminal closes.</p>

  <h2 id="links">{names.urlScheme}:// links</h2>
  <p><code>{names.urlScheme}://open?path=Plan.md&amp;line=12</code> opens a file in Jotra's folder at a line, from a browser or another program, and <code>{names.urlScheme}://new</code> opens a new tab.{names.isCurrentName && <Fragment> <code>writ://</code> links work the same way.</Fragment>} A link opens only a file inside the folder, and following one writes nothing.</p>
</Prose>
